n about a symbol.\" with name like 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo. It's probably scoped to the codebase (httpie). Let me query \"httpie.output.formatters.colors.get_lexer\" and \"get_lexer\".\n\nLet me make the calls.\n\nAfter that, write the final test code.\n\nOne more consideration on test correctness: `get_lexer('text/html')` \u2014 HtmlLexer mimetypes: ['text/html', 'application/xhtml+xml']. Yes.\n\nAlso, is there any chance that 'application/vnd.foo+xml' matches some lexer mimetype? Unlikely.\n\n'foo/json' \u2014 no mimetype match. 'foo/bar' \u2014 no. 'x-foo/python' \u2014 no. 'x/bar+xml' \u2014 no.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtle thing about pygments' `get_lexer_for_mimetype`: in pygments 2.x, the function signature is `get_lexer_for_mimetype(_mime, **options)`. It iterates `_iter_lexerclasses()`. Hmm, but there's also this in newer pygments (2.10+?):\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer_for_mimetype(_mime, **options):\n    for lexer in _iter_lexerclasses():\n        for mime in lexer.mimetypes:\n            if _mime == mime:\n                return lexer(**options)\n    raise ClassNotFound('...')\n```\n\nYes.\n\nAnother subtlety: TextLexer \u2014 when you call `get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/plain')`, you get TextLexer instance. isinstance check works.\n\nBut wait \u2014 in httpie's actual code, is it `isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)`? Yes per snippet.\n\nNow, JSON body parsing: `json.loads(body)` \u2014 body='{\"a\": 1}' parses fine.\n\nEdge: body='not json' \u2192 ValueError. Good. Note: json.loads('not json') raises ValueError (JSONDecodeError subclasses ValueError). Good.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `get_lexer` might be defined with different default args in the real code: `def get_lexer(mime: str, explicit_json=False, body='')`. The snippet shows that. OK.\n\nLet me now also double-check: in the real httpie code, the function is:\n\n```python\ndef get_lexer(\n    mime: str,\n    explicit_json=False,\n    body=''\n) -> Optional[Type[Lexer]]:\n    # Build candidate mime type and lexer names.\n    mime_types, lexer_names = [mime], []\n    type_, subtype = mime.split('/', 1)\n    if '+' not in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append(subtype)\n    else:\n        subtype_name, subtype_suffix = subtype.split('+', 1)\n        lexer_names.extend([subtype_name, subtype_suffix])\n        mime_types.extend([\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_name),\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_suffix)\n        ])\n\n    # As a last resort, if no lexer feels responsible, and\n    # the subtype contains 'json', take the JSON lexer\n    if 'json' in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append('json')\n\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n\n    if explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)):\n        # JSON response with an incorrect Content-Type?\n        try:\n            json.loads(body)  # FIXME: the body also gets parsed in json.py\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # Nope\n        else:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n\n    return lexer\n```\n\nYes, that matches httpie's source.\n\nNote: the return type annotation says Optional[Type[Lexer]] but actually returns instances. Whatever.\n\nNow, one potential gotcha: `get_lexer_by_name('json')` returns JSONLexer instance. `isinstance(lexer, JsonLexer)` \u2014 JsonLexer is in pygments.lexers.data. Good.\n\nAnother gotcha: For 'application/hal+json', if pygments version has 'application/hal+json' registered? I checked JSONLexer mimetypes in pygments 2.15: ['application/json', 'application/json-ld', 'application/x-json', 'a